<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peace of Prague (1635)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1635">1635</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Peace of Prague was concluded on 30 May 1635 between Emperor Ferdinand II and Elector John George I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, and most Protestant princes of the Empire soon joined it. Saxony gained Upper and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-lusatia">Lower Lusatia</a>, the Edict of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repatriation-of-cultural-property">Restitution</a> was effectively suspended, and the princes' armies were to unite under the emperor. It failed to end the war, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> continued.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peace-of-prague-1635.jpg" alt="Peace of Prague (1635)" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frans Luycx · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann_Georg_I_Saxony.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> began in 1618 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-v-of-the-palatinate">Frederick V of the Palatinate</a> accepted the crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. Emperor Ferdinand II suppressed the Bohemian Revolt with Bavarian support, and in 1622 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-i-elector-of-bavaria">Maximilian I, Elector of Bavaria</a>, invaded the Palatinate and sent Frederick into exile. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-iv-of-denmark">Christian IV of Denmark</a> invaded northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> for the Protestant cause until he withdrew in 1629.</p><p>Ferdinand then issued the Edict of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repatriation-of-cultural-property">Restitution</a>, which required property transferred since 1552 to be restored to its original owner, nearly always the Catholic Church. By effectively undoing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-augsburg">Peace of Augsburg</a> of 1555, it pushed moderate Protestants such as John George of Saxony and George William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> into opposition.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> in 1630. After his death at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lutzen-1632">Lützen</a> in 1632, Sweden formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heilbronn-league">Heilbronn League</a>, which lost at Nördlingen in 1634. From February 1633 Ferdinand had accepted that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> could not be imposed by force and opened talks on amending the Edict. Mediators from Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a> helped agree a draft in November 1634.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty was a bilateral agreement between Ferdinand and John George, and other states joined later. Eight days of negotiation took place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-castle">Prague Castle</a>, where the Defenestrations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> had begun the war in 1618. The Edict of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repatriation-of-cultural-property">Restitution</a> was effectively revoked, with 12 November 1627 set as the date for returning properties, though Protestant administrators of former Catholic prince-bishoprics and imperial abbeys were excluded from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a>.</p><p>Alliances between states of the Empire, or with outside powers, were prohibited, which dissolved the Catholic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heilbronn-league">Heilbronn Leagues</a>. The armies of the states were in principle unified as the Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, but this proved almost impossible to enforce. A general amnesty covered those who had fought the Imperial troops, apart from the descendants of Frederick V.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Many states acceded, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a>, Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>. Württemberg was excluded and Strasbourg did not accede. William V of Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a> acceded but in 1636 allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and was put under an imperial ban. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, which had occupied Lower and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-lusatia">Upper Lusatia</a> in 1623, received both in perpetuity under the Traditionsrezess of 1636.</p><p>It is generally agreed that the peace ended the war as primarily one of religion. Fighting continued, however. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> on 19 May 1635 and entered the war as an active belligerent, and in 1642 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> won at Breitenfeld and overran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. Peace came only with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> in 1648.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peace of Prague (1635)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Thirty Years’ War John George I first stayed neutral, then joined Gustavus Adolphus of Sweden; the Saxon contingent fled at the Battle of Breitenfeld in 1631, and Saxony suffered heavily from both sides before its separate <b>Peace of Prague</b> with the emperor in 1635.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Peace of Prague</b> of 1635 transferred Lower Lusatia from Bohemia to the Electorate of Saxony.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-lower-lusatia">Margraviate of Lower Lusatia</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Peace of Prague</b> of 1635 transferred Upper Lusatia to the Electorate of Saxony.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-upper-lusatia">Margraviate of Upper Lusatia</a></li></ul></section>
